WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:26.193
- Hi everyone.

00:00:26.193 --> 00:00:29.810
Welcome to the daily homeroom livestream.

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:31.840
For those of you all who
are wondering what this is,

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:34.400
this is a series of
conversations that we've started

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:35.430
over the last few months.

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:37.040
It was I guess, catalyzed by COVID.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:39.330
But it's a way of us staying in contact

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:41.660
and having conversations about education,

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:43.240
what's going on in the broader world.

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:45.100
And I'm super excited for the conversation

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:46.950
we're gonna have in a few minutes

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:48.810
with a guest that when we scheduled it,

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:50.930
we didn't realize how relevant it would be

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:53.720
given what's going on in the world.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:55.510
But before we jump into that conversation,

00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:57.220
I do wanna give a special shout out

00:00:57.220 --> 00:00:59.070
to several corporate
partners who stepped up

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:00.550
as soon as they realized that Khan Academy

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:02.570
was already running
into deficit pre-COVID,

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:04.760
and that as our traffic has gone up

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:07.740
by a factor of 2, 2.5, 3X,

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:09.800
we've tried to accelerate more programs

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:11.850
to support teachers, parents and students,

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:14.780
they stepped up to help
us close some of that gap.

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:17.868
Special thanks to Bank of
America, Google.org, AT&amp;T,

00:01:17.868 --> 00:01:19.700
Fastly, and Norvatis and
the many other supporters

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:20.780
of Khan Academy.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:23.890
I just wanna make it very
clear, every donation matters.

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:25.180
We're still running at a deficit.

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:27.070
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:29.640
please think about making that donation.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:31.080
So with that said,

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:34.240
I'm eager to introduce
our guest for today,

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:37.500
Mayor Sam Liccardo, mayor of San Jose.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:40.900
A very large city, a major
city here in Silicon Valley,

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:42.520
the largest city that I'm close to.

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:43.353
I'm here in Mountain View,

00:01:43.353 --> 00:01:45.450
which is probably about six or seven miles

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:47.080
away from San Jose.

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:49.960
So I consider myself
a constituent of sorts

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:51.950
of Mayor Liccardo.

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:54.130
Mayor Liccardo thanks so much for joining,

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:57.170
especially considering,
I'm sure a lot of things

00:01:57.170 --> 00:01:59.510
going on in your life right now.

00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:01.170
- Yes, it's a challenging moment.

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:02.140
But I should say Sal,

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:04.410
thank you for all that you
and Khan Academy are doing

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:07.050
for literally millions
of kids in our country

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:08.320
and throughout the world.

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:10.160
We're so appreciative.

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:10.993
- No, no.

00:02:10.993 --> 00:02:12.859
And maybe a good place to start.

00:02:12.859 --> 00:02:14.500
As I said, when we scheduled this,

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:16.010
we thought we're gonna talk about COVID

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:17.190
and maybe the economy

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:21.000
and how cities are thinking
about school, education,

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.460
but obviously, given what's
going on in the world

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:26.040
around George Floyd and the protests,

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:29.720
and I guess I would just love
your perspective as a mayor

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:33.530
of a major American city,
how are you processing it

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:37.100
as individual and what
levers do you think cities,

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:40.020
or state, or federal government has

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:41.823
to kind of help us navigate this?

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:45.150
- Well, I think as you're saying,

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:48.463
the action really is in the cities.

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:52.690
That is certain that every
police department is local.

00:02:52.690 --> 00:02:53.720
We're probably not gonna get

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:56.730
much national leadership on
this given some of the comments

00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:58.280
that come out of the White House.

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:00.660
And so, really mayors
are mightily challenged

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:02.380
along with police chief's,

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:05.570
to really see how we
can meaningfully address

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:06.883
the calls for reform that are out there.

00:03:06.883 --> 00:03:11.010
There is a righteous cries for change,

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:16.000
and certainly they are
reflective of what is a decades

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:19.590
if not centuries old problem
we have had with race

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:20.920
and equity in this country.

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:24.040
And so, the underlying
challenges are gonna take

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:26.050
a lot more time to solve,

00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:30.420
and I think they're fundamentally
racial and economic.

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:32.730
But as we look at this manifestation

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:34.587
that is with the use of
force and police departments

00:03:34.587 --> 00:03:37.860
and so forth, this is an
opportunity for a lot of cities,

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:42.110
I think, to step up in ways
to be able to demonstrate

00:03:42.110 --> 00:03:45.033
that we are hearing clearly
the cries for change

00:03:45.033 --> 00:03:49.090
and to utilize more innovative approaches

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:50.660
that have been used in the past.

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:52.167
I'll give you a quick
example here in San Jose,

00:03:52.167 --> 00:03:53.650
and we're by no means perfect.

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:56.470
We are struggling mightily
with this as everyone else is,

00:03:56.470 --> 00:03:59.800
but we've been really
trying to leverage data

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:00.970
as much as possible,

00:04:00.970 --> 00:04:03.500
recording literally
every single police stop,

00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:06.600
every detention, every
arrest, every questioning,

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:09.300
and the demographic
information of each individual.

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:12.380
And then we make that
public and we release it

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:14.270
for outside experts to study.

00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:16.620
One of these reports
just came out last week.

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:18.150
They analyze how we're using force

00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:21.320
relative to our arrest
rates in each demographic,

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:23.060
and whether or not our use of force

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:24.490
is getting more severe or less severe.

00:04:24.490 --> 00:04:26.780
Fortunately, it's becoming less severe.

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:29.440
And really help us
understand how we can change,

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:30.290
how we can improve.

00:04:30.290 --> 00:04:34.090
And so, I think being able
to be more transparent

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:36.640
and more accountable is
really a critical first step,

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:38.010
and that's something that most cities

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:39.300
can do relatively quickly.

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:41.060
Obviously, much bigger challenge

00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:43.530
is addressing this fundamental challenge

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:44.900
we have around race in our country,

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:48.920
which we could have a very long
conversation about, I know.

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:52.420
- Right, and just focused on
this one issue of use of force,

00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:54.950
which is obviously the catalyst
or the center of all this

00:04:54.950 --> 00:04:58.560
and obviously there's layers
to that onion as you refer to,

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:03.560
what do you think, I guess how serious,

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:09.300
how prevalent is this in police forces?

00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:13.690
What were mayors and Sheriffs
thinking about before this?

00:05:13.690 --> 00:05:15.570
'Cause obviously this isn't the first.

00:05:15.570 --> 00:05:17.550
It seems like every few
months, there's a case.

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:18.920
Some of them are with the police,

00:05:18.920 --> 00:05:23.740
some of them are a case of some vigilante

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:26.960
doing something that to many of us

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:29.230
seem race based, but then it doesn't seem

00:05:29.230 --> 00:05:31.000
like justice is served.

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:35.860
What were cities doing
before and how do you think

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:38.810
they should approach change

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:42.610
in both how the
police/citizen interaction,

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:45.460
and then also in the criminal
justice system beyond that?

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:49.250
- Yeah, what's interesting
is I think there have been

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:54.240
a lot of good changes in
recent years in large cities.

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:57.500
Despite the fact what we're
seeing clearly in the media is,

00:05:57.500 --> 00:05:59.380
this is still happening way too often

00:06:00.621 --> 00:06:05.130
and we know George Floyd,
sadly, won't be the last.

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:08.050
We have seen this happen so often.

00:06:08.050 --> 00:06:12.650
And we are desperate hoping
that this will be the last

00:06:12.650 --> 00:06:15.430
but we know that there is a
fundamental issue of culture

00:06:15.430 --> 00:06:18.400
that needs to change in an
awful lot of departments

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:23.383
in terms of racist attitudes
around use of force.

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:27.710
But I think what is also
particularly challenging

00:06:27.710 --> 00:06:31.280
as we see the widespread
demonstrations and protests,

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:35.190
is how you police a large gathering

00:06:35.190 --> 00:06:38.220
where 99% of the participants
are peacefully expressing

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:39.820
their first amendment rights.

00:06:39.820 --> 00:06:42.500
And yet you know, and this is
true in virtually every city,

00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:44.020
'cause I'm having these text exchanges

00:06:44.020 --> 00:06:46.510
with all my colleagues and
mayors throughout the country,

00:06:46.510 --> 00:06:47.963
very progressive mayors.

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:53.840
None of them want to see
anything but dramatic change.

00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:56.130
But they are frustrated
because in each city,

00:06:56.130 --> 00:07:00.260
you have provocateurs,
folks who are instigating

00:07:00.260 --> 00:07:03.570
and provoking to try to
get a police response.

00:07:03.570 --> 00:07:07.180
And maybe it's throwing bottles or debris.

00:07:07.180 --> 00:07:09.590
Maybe it's blowing up
fireworks over the heads

00:07:09.590 --> 00:07:11.110
of the officers.

00:07:11.110 --> 00:07:13.500
Maybe it's looting a whole host of things.

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:15.730
And the officers are really being put

00:07:15.730 --> 00:07:18.090
in an incredibly difficult position.

00:07:18.090 --> 00:07:20.880
How do you keep everybody safe,
how do you protect the city,

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:23.630
and at the same time,

00:07:23.630 --> 00:07:26.713
how do you do it
constitutionally, and fairly?

00:07:28.070 --> 00:07:30.640
So very difficult issues
that an awful lot of folks

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:31.590
are grappling with.

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:36.290
I am bullish on the ability
of an awful lot of mayors

00:07:36.290 --> 00:07:39.170
and police chiefs to
step up to this challenge

00:07:39.170 --> 00:07:40.030
in the long term.

00:07:40.030 --> 00:07:42.380
In the short term, I
think this is much harder.

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:46.260
- And those are two
really interesting things

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:47.230
that I would love to double click.

00:07:47.230 --> 00:07:49.320
And for anyone watching
on Facebook or YouTube,

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:50.153
please ask questions.

00:07:50.153 --> 00:07:51.920
I see some questions coming in already.

00:07:51.920 --> 00:07:55.970
I'll get them to the mayor
as much as we have time for

00:07:55.970 --> 00:07:58.930
but on the first question,
you say there's some culture,

00:07:58.930 --> 00:08:02.440
there are some deep
racism in certain pockets

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:05.630
of various institutions,
including law enforcement.

00:08:05.630 --> 00:08:08.130
How do you change culture?

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:09.050
'Cause that feels like something

00:08:09.050 --> 00:08:11.420
you can't just change a policy.

00:08:11.420 --> 00:08:13.180
I mean, you can do things like body cams

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:15.940
and things like that, so you
can monitor better use of data,

00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:17.740
but how do you actually change culture?

00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:20.360
What are mayors, sheriffs...

00:08:20.360 --> 00:08:22.210
What can the police force themselves do,

00:08:22.210 --> 00:08:25.420
what can the community do
to help change that culture?

00:08:25.420 --> 00:08:28.580
- Yeah, and that's a really
challenging question,

00:08:28.580 --> 00:08:30.770
because it's not just police departments.

00:08:30.770 --> 00:08:32.340
Racism, white supremacy,

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:35.110
this is a history that has been with us.

00:08:35.110 --> 00:08:37.620
It is in so many institutions,

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:40.640
every institution, really, in our country.

00:08:40.640 --> 00:08:43.240
It just so happens when
it's in a police department,

00:08:44.170 --> 00:08:46.690
the consequences are deadly.

00:08:46.690 --> 00:08:50.760
And so, frankly, we all have
to be a little more honest

00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:53.320
with ourselves about this reality.

00:08:53.320 --> 00:08:56.720
that it's not just the cop
that deserves scrutiny,

00:08:56.720 --> 00:08:59.030
but it's certainly the cop because we know

00:08:59.030 --> 00:09:01.310
they're the ones who are
authorized to use deadly force

00:09:01.310 --> 00:09:02.233
in our community.

00:09:03.490 --> 00:09:06.130
What departments like San Jose are doing

00:09:06.130 --> 00:09:07.830
starting three or four
years ago, you ask me

00:09:07.830 --> 00:09:10.660
we were one of the first cities
to deploy body worn cameras

00:09:10.660 --> 00:09:12.360
and lots of cities are
doing that now and so forth.

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:15.610
But really trying to be intentional

00:09:15.610 --> 00:09:18.780
around bias based policing,
we now have training

00:09:18.780 --> 00:09:22.510
in every academy and
throughout the career,

00:09:22.510 --> 00:09:26.630
every officer here, around
bias and hidden bias,

00:09:26.630 --> 00:09:29.400
around the deescalation of violence,

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:31.500
around what we call
critical incident training,

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:33.440
which is really focused on folks

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:34.830
having mental health episodes,

00:09:34.830 --> 00:09:37.870
because we know way too
many of those encounters

00:09:37.870 --> 00:09:39.770
are resulting in deadly force,

00:09:39.770 --> 00:09:41.830
and trying to ensure we can de-escalate

00:09:41.830 --> 00:09:44.170
and get mental health
professionals in there,

00:09:44.170 --> 00:09:46.740
so that it's not somebody
with a badge and a gun

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:47.890
who's having that first encounter

00:09:47.890 --> 00:09:50.610
of somebody having a
mental health episode.

00:09:50.610 --> 00:09:53.680
And by the way, those
are in way too frequently

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:54.870
in communities of color,

00:09:54.870 --> 00:09:58.530
because of lack of access
to mental health resources.

00:09:58.530 --> 00:10:00.910
So there's a lot that needs to be done.

00:10:00.910 --> 00:10:02.530
Those are just three training modules

00:10:02.530 --> 00:10:05.339
that we've incorporated now
for every single officer

00:10:05.339 --> 00:10:07.960
in the academy and thereafter.

00:10:07.960 --> 00:10:12.160
And it has to be a focus
on continuous improvement.

00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:15.440
Because I like to believe we
are one of the most progressive

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:17.780
departments if not the
most in the country,

00:10:17.780 --> 00:10:19.620
and we've got our own failures.

00:10:19.620 --> 00:10:23.060
And so there has to be an
attitude around focus of,

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:23.893
we're not perfect,

00:10:23.893 --> 00:10:25.137
and we just got to keep pushing.

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:29.490
- And what levers are there?

00:10:29.490 --> 00:10:32.757
I mean, those all sound
like very powerful ones.

00:10:32.757 --> 00:10:34.540
I think one of the things
that was disturbing

00:10:34.540 --> 00:10:36.600
about the George Floyd incident,

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:40.500
tragedy was that not only
did you have the one officer,

00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:41.333
kneeling on him,

00:10:41.333 --> 00:10:42.730
but then you have several other officers

00:10:42.730 --> 00:10:44.370
who kind of stand by and watch

00:10:44.370 --> 00:10:46.840
and kind of allow it to happen.

00:10:46.840 --> 00:10:50.088
How does that culture evolve
over time so that it's not,

00:10:50.088 --> 00:10:53.970
even the more neutral
or the police officers

00:10:53.970 --> 00:10:55.880
who wouldn't have done those
types of things on their own,

00:10:55.880 --> 00:11:00.186
but they either don't
feel, don't see the space

00:11:00.186 --> 00:11:02.940
to confront their peer,

00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:05.090
or maybe they're afraid
to for some reason.

00:11:06.030 --> 00:11:07.860
I know these are very hard questions,

00:11:07.860 --> 00:11:10.480
but how do you get to the
root of that type of culture?

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:11.930
- Those are really important questions,

00:11:11.930 --> 00:11:14.240
but we expect police departments

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:17.310
to be paramilitary
organizations that will follow

00:11:17.310 --> 00:11:20.360
a chain of authority.

00:11:20.360 --> 00:11:22.710
Because when you put
people into situations

00:11:22.710 --> 00:11:26.460
where they're under the risk
of serious harm or death

00:11:26.460 --> 00:11:29.400
as officers are way too
often in our community

00:11:29.400 --> 00:11:31.860
we all live in a violent country,

00:11:31.860 --> 00:11:34.210
and this is what officers
are subjected to.

00:11:34.210 --> 00:11:36.380
That means they have to follow orders.

00:11:36.380 --> 00:11:39.300
And follow that chain of command.

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:40.330
At the same time,

00:11:40.330 --> 00:11:42.730
we expect and should expect them

00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:45.830
to step out of that chain
and correct one another,

00:11:45.830 --> 00:11:47.570
admonish one another, and say,

00:11:47.570 --> 00:11:50.000
Hey get off when it appears that somebody

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:51.033
is choking to death.

00:11:52.300 --> 00:11:55.330
Those may be conflicting tensions,

00:11:55.330 --> 00:11:57.640
and I can't say I'm an expert in policing,

00:11:57.640 --> 00:11:59.940
but I think we have to be honest

00:11:59.940 --> 00:12:02.750
about our expectations of the police,

00:12:02.750 --> 00:12:05.040
at the same time that we're
certainly demanding more

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:08.343
from them and expecting
greater consequence as well.

00:12:09.690 --> 00:12:10.590
- And to your second point,

00:12:10.590 --> 00:12:12.930
I mean, I completely I could imagine,

00:12:12.930 --> 00:12:15.150
many police officers and sheriffs

00:12:15.150 --> 00:12:17.060
are really struggling with,

00:12:17.060 --> 00:12:18.040
where is that line?

00:12:18.040 --> 00:12:21.440
How do you stop looters but
allow for the peaceful protests?

00:12:21.440 --> 00:12:23.030
What about the in between situations

00:12:23.030 --> 00:12:25.240
where people aren't looting
or doing something illegal,

00:12:25.240 --> 00:12:28.526
but maybe they are very
actively antagonizing

00:12:28.526 --> 00:12:30.850
hoping for a reaction?

00:12:30.850 --> 00:12:33.660
What are those conversations
like with your Sheriff

00:12:33.660 --> 00:12:35.450
with the police force right now?

00:12:35.450 --> 00:12:37.400
How are they processing it?

00:12:37.400 --> 00:12:38.600
I guess on both fronts,

00:12:38.600 --> 00:12:40.480
how are they internalizing
some of the change

00:12:40.480 --> 00:12:43.120
that needs to happen and
then how are they trying

00:12:43.120 --> 00:12:47.420
to thread that needle of
protecting the protests

00:12:47.420 --> 00:12:50.783
and also protecting property
and keeping people safe?

00:12:51.820 --> 00:12:54.920
- It is challenging and our
police chief here in San Jose

00:12:54.920 --> 00:12:56.870
we're city a little more
than a million people,

00:12:56.870 --> 00:12:58.130
the 10th largest in the country

00:12:58.130 --> 00:13:00.550
and so we have big city
challenges and tensions

00:13:00.550 --> 00:13:01.720
and we're very diverse.

00:13:01.720 --> 00:13:03.410
And our police chief Eddie Garcia

00:13:03.410 --> 00:13:06.770
came to our city as a
child not speaking English.

00:13:06.770 --> 00:13:10.440
So he has a certain grasp certainly of,

00:13:10.440 --> 00:13:14.240
the intensity of feeling and concern

00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:16.580
that's coming from many
corners of our community.

00:13:16.580 --> 00:13:17.430
At the same time,

00:13:17.430 --> 00:13:21.330
I can tell you he is being
absolutely harshly criticized

00:13:21.330 --> 00:13:25.450
because we've got officers out
there encountering protesters

00:13:25.450 --> 00:13:28.210
who are protesting abuse of
use of force by the police,

00:13:28.210 --> 00:13:29.170
at the same time,

00:13:29.170 --> 00:13:31.250
that we are resorting to tear gas,

00:13:31.250 --> 00:13:32.920
and yes rubber bullets.

00:13:32.920 --> 00:13:34.810
And I have my own very serious concern

00:13:34.810 --> 00:13:36.080
about the use of rubber bullets,

00:13:36.080 --> 00:13:38.580
for example in crowd management.

00:13:38.580 --> 00:13:41.910
But that is used widely in
virtually every big city

00:13:41.910 --> 00:13:43.500
in this country.

00:13:43.500 --> 00:13:46.520
And so these are really
hard conversations,

00:13:46.520 --> 00:13:47.860
they take time,

00:13:47.860 --> 00:13:51.510
and they take an ability to reflect

00:13:51.510 --> 00:13:54.450
and an ability for folks to take a breath.

00:13:54.450 --> 00:13:56.060
And unfortunately,

00:13:56.060 --> 00:13:57.340
that's not this moment.

00:13:57.340 --> 00:13:59.960
Right now we've got a
lot of folks reacting,

00:13:59.960 --> 00:14:04.320
and it's hard to have meaningful
conversations about change,

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:06.100
at the same time that folks are reacting

00:14:06.100 --> 00:14:08.823
to the minute to minute stress.

00:14:09.806 --> 00:14:13.850
- Right, now there's some
questions about that from YouTube.

00:14:13.850 --> 00:14:16.970
Aditya Gupta, just wrote a
question about how do you have

00:14:16.970 --> 00:14:17.910
change to your point?

00:14:17.910 --> 00:14:19.938
You know, right now there's,

00:14:19.938 --> 00:14:21.970
we're in a highly heightened
state emotional state.

00:14:21.970 --> 00:14:23.590
People are angry,

00:14:23.590 --> 00:14:26.390
often it's kind of like
they've been seeing patterns

00:14:26.390 --> 00:14:29.150
over multiple years now.

00:14:29.150 --> 00:14:31.500
And there's also a
sense that for everyone,

00:14:31.500 --> 00:14:33.810
you see how many hundreds might happen

00:14:33.810 --> 00:14:35.460
that you don't see that don't get

00:14:35.460 --> 00:14:38.740
happen to get captured on a phone camera?

00:14:38.740 --> 00:14:40.380
And then for every one of those,

00:14:40.380 --> 00:14:42.270
how many of those don't end up deadly

00:14:42.270 --> 00:14:43.860
but could still end up,

00:14:43.860 --> 00:14:47.360
quite humiliating or dehumanizing.

00:14:47.360 --> 00:14:48.903
And so Aditya asks,

00:14:50.377 --> 00:14:51.910
"hi Mayor Liccardo,

00:14:51.910 --> 00:14:54.290
what do you think is the
best way to voice change

00:14:54.290 --> 00:14:55.290
in our country?

00:14:55.290 --> 00:14:57.120
Is it protesting, petitions,

00:14:57.120 --> 00:14:59.660
I don't want violence but I
do want effective change."

00:14:59.660 --> 00:15:01.320
I think Aditya is kind of voicing

00:15:01.320 --> 00:15:04.640
what frankly almost every
American is thinking right now.

00:15:04.640 --> 00:15:09.250
- Well, hate to trade on familiar ground

00:15:09.250 --> 00:15:13.200
and sound a little too pollyannish.

00:15:13.200 --> 00:15:15.660
But get out there and register to vote

00:15:15.660 --> 00:15:18.180
and get other people to register to vote.

00:15:18.180 --> 00:15:21.020
And, get folks to register for the census

00:15:21.020 --> 00:15:23.500
and get engaged in the hard change.

00:15:23.500 --> 00:15:25.790
I think, look there's no question that

00:15:25.790 --> 00:15:27.720
the demands for change are righteous

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:29.330
coming from protesters in this city

00:15:29.330 --> 00:15:30.630
and throughout the country.

00:15:30.630 --> 00:15:34.360
At the same time, the
expectations that we're going

00:15:34.360 --> 00:15:38.860
to suddenly move and change
major institutions overnight

00:15:38.860 --> 00:15:41.120
has never been the reality.

00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:43.660
It takes hard work in a democracy.

00:15:43.660 --> 00:15:45.402
And it means people have to get engaged

00:15:45.402 --> 00:15:47.100
through the ballot box

00:15:47.100 --> 00:15:49.160
engaged in the city councils,

00:15:49.160 --> 00:15:54.030
engaged in getting people who
are good to go run for office,

00:15:54.030 --> 00:15:56.853
who have the values that
are consistent with theirs.

00:15:58.030 --> 00:16:00.820
I hate to get too partisan here.

00:16:00.820 --> 00:16:05.070
But we do have a real challenge
in national leadership

00:16:05.070 --> 00:16:06.570
in this country at this moment.

00:16:06.570 --> 00:16:08.530
And we can all imagine
how this conversation

00:16:08.530 --> 00:16:11.110
might go differently, if
we had the prior president

00:16:11.110 --> 00:16:12.950
rather than this one.

00:16:12.950 --> 00:16:14.950
There's only one way
we're gonna change that.

00:16:14.950 --> 00:16:17.670
And that means we gotta get
a lot of people registered,

00:16:17.670 --> 00:16:22.330
because it's by no means
a forsaken conclusion

00:16:22.330 --> 00:16:23.880
what this election is gonna be.

00:16:24.780 --> 00:16:27.378
- And what's your sense
of protests themselves?

00:16:27.378 --> 00:16:29.180
When you just think about
it intellectually like,

00:16:29.180 --> 00:16:31.190
Okay well, it shows solidarity,

00:16:31.190 --> 00:16:33.180
it shows a lot of people care about this.

00:16:33.180 --> 00:16:34.940
But what do you think is
going to be the impact

00:16:34.940 --> 00:16:36.250
of the protests themselves?

00:16:36.250 --> 00:16:38.960
Maybe it does activate
more people to vote.

00:16:38.960 --> 00:16:40.609
But does it have an impact?

00:16:40.609 --> 00:16:43.070
You know, you're in City Hall,

00:16:43.070 --> 00:16:44.940
you're talking to a lot of the player?

00:16:44.940 --> 00:16:47.103
Does it change thinking in your mind?

00:16:48.340 --> 00:16:50.860
- Yeah, I think it does.

00:16:50.860 --> 00:16:52.920
There's no question that it's opening eyes

00:16:52.920 --> 00:16:55.430
in my conversations with my colleagues

00:16:55.430 --> 00:16:58.260
mayors throughout the country
and my own police chief

00:16:58.260 --> 00:17:01.173
and others that I think
people are understanding

00:17:01.173 --> 00:17:05.940
the depth of the pain in ways
that perhaps we didn't before.

00:17:05.940 --> 00:17:10.360
But let me also be blatantly
political for a moment

00:17:10.360 --> 00:17:12.040
and coldly calculating,

00:17:12.040 --> 00:17:16.313
I am very concerned about
how this narrative plays out.

00:17:17.630 --> 00:17:19.850
In perhaps those suburban communities

00:17:19.850 --> 00:17:22.280
that may not be as sympathetic.

00:17:22.280 --> 00:17:25.710
Where there those on one side essentially

00:17:25.710 --> 00:17:30.240
use this to demonstrate
why we need more harsh,

00:17:32.690 --> 00:17:37.690
militaristic approach to law
enforcement in this country,

00:17:37.980 --> 00:17:41.120
to contain the looting and the violence

00:17:41.120 --> 00:17:45.890
and all the other ramifications
of having those folks

00:17:45.890 --> 00:17:48.340
who are out there provoking
that kind of response

00:17:49.350 --> 00:17:50.210
within the crowds.

00:17:50.210 --> 00:17:52.140
Obviously more than 99% of folks

00:17:52.140 --> 00:17:53.830
are there for the right reason.

00:17:53.830 --> 00:17:55.923
It's those 50 or 100
folks that are creating

00:17:55.923 --> 00:17:58.310
the huge challenge in every city

00:17:58.310 --> 00:18:01.063
and I'm very concerned about
the long term implications

00:18:01.063 --> 00:18:05.640
of what this means for
those suburban voters,

00:18:05.640 --> 00:18:09.100
particularly as we go in
this next national election.

00:18:09.100 --> 00:18:12.320
- Yeah, it's a complex issue for sure.

00:18:12.320 --> 00:18:14.400
And, maybe we'll go one layer out,

00:18:14.400 --> 00:18:17.120
because when we first
scheduled this conversation,

00:18:17.120 --> 00:18:18.740
obviously we didn't know
that this was going to be

00:18:18.740 --> 00:18:20.893
the state of affairs in the world.

00:18:20.893 --> 00:18:24.350
And I think, COVID is the underlying thing

00:18:24.350 --> 00:18:27.450
and one of the aspects of COVID is,

00:18:27.450 --> 00:18:30.330
its kind of heightened a
lot of economic inequality.

00:18:30.330 --> 00:18:31.580
And to your point,

00:18:31.580 --> 00:18:33.060
a lot of what we're talking about,

00:18:33.060 --> 00:18:37.060
even with the protests and
all of that surrounding it,

00:18:37.060 --> 00:18:39.030
there is an underlying,

00:18:39.030 --> 00:18:40.370
there's a race aspect,

00:18:40.370 --> 00:18:42.400
and then mingled with
that is there's kind of

00:18:42.400 --> 00:18:44.670
an economic aspect of it.

00:18:44.670 --> 00:18:46.420
How do you think,

00:18:46.420 --> 00:18:48.150
I guess how do mayors like yourself,

00:18:48.150 --> 00:18:49.160
think about this issue?

00:18:49.160 --> 00:18:52.360
How do y'all, what levers
are there at your disposal

00:18:52.360 --> 00:18:55.320
for addressing economic inequality,

00:18:55.320 --> 00:18:57.310
or even educational inequality

00:18:57.310 --> 00:19:00.360
or maybe issues like racism generally?

00:19:00.360 --> 00:19:04.283
- Yeah, there's no question
at the root of this.

00:19:05.720 --> 00:19:07.350
We are seeing the issue played out

00:19:07.350 --> 00:19:08.670
around use of force and police,

00:19:08.670 --> 00:19:12.550
but at the root of this
issues of racial equity

00:19:12.550 --> 00:19:16.030
and economics and the fact
that poverty in this country

00:19:16.030 --> 00:19:18.970
is too closely correlated to race.

00:19:18.970 --> 00:19:22.090
And I believe that is the root of much

00:19:22.090 --> 00:19:23.540
of what we're grappling with.

00:19:24.570 --> 00:19:28.100
And we saw that laid bare,
as you said, by COVID,

00:19:28.100 --> 00:19:30.420
and we saw the disparities
even more impactful

00:19:30.420 --> 00:19:32.040
in terms of health outcomes,

00:19:32.040 --> 00:19:34.410
a latin next populations,
Afro American populations

00:19:34.410 --> 00:19:37.800
that are dying at higher rates
being affected higher rates.

00:19:37.800 --> 00:19:39.940
I'm trying to see how we can use

00:19:41.050 --> 00:19:44.430
not use but find
opportunity in this crisis,

00:19:44.430 --> 00:19:47.070
and by the crisis now I'm
just referring to pandemic

00:19:47.070 --> 00:19:48.260
for a moment.

00:19:48.260 --> 00:19:49.840
You know as we think about the ability

00:19:49.840 --> 00:19:52.970
to use emergency orders to
move more quickly to mobilize,

00:19:52.970 --> 00:19:55.850
for example, we've got a huge
homelessness challenge here

00:19:55.850 --> 00:19:57.560
as every city does in California

00:19:57.560 --> 00:19:59.270
and throughout the West Coast.

00:19:59.270 --> 00:20:02.090
And so we're using some
of the emergency orders

00:20:02.090 --> 00:20:04.810
to be able to streamline construction

00:20:04.810 --> 00:20:08.260
of prefabricated modular
housing at a relatively low cost

00:20:08.260 --> 00:20:12.327
maybe $30-40,000 per unit
where it normally costs

00:20:12.327 --> 00:20:15.910
$700,000 to build the unit in
a typical apartment building,

00:20:15.910 --> 00:20:18.980
and we'll be able to build
some of these projects now

00:20:18.980 --> 00:20:20.560
that we've gotten underway in four weeks

00:20:20.560 --> 00:20:22.640
it ordinarily take four years.

00:20:22.640 --> 00:20:25.800
You get about 500 homeless
people housed this way.

00:20:25.800 --> 00:20:28.290
This is how we can find
opportunity in this crisis

00:20:28.290 --> 00:20:31.070
as we think about the digital
divide that is so impactful,

00:20:31.070 --> 00:20:32.443
particularly when all the important work

00:20:32.443 --> 00:20:35.150
that Khan Academy's is doing right now.

00:20:35.150 --> 00:20:39.810
Again, using the ability to
push red tape out of the way

00:20:39.810 --> 00:20:43.410
to be able to more
rapidly deploy free WiFi

00:20:43.410 --> 00:20:47.180
right now we've got a lot of
interesting projects going on.

00:20:47.180 --> 00:20:48.340
Very large school district,

00:20:48.340 --> 00:20:50.690
our east part of our city where we have

00:20:50.690 --> 00:20:54.310
very high population of
families without internet access

00:20:54.310 --> 00:20:57.150
right here in the heart
of Silicon Valley sadly.

00:20:57.150 --> 00:20:59.330
And we just connected one community

00:20:59.330 --> 00:21:02.680
around James Lick High School
6,000 families with free WiFi.

00:21:02.680 --> 00:21:04.120
In a few weeks we'll be able to do that

00:21:04.120 --> 00:21:06.540
with another community
around another high school

00:21:06.540 --> 00:21:07.373
in eastern Jose.

00:21:07.373 --> 00:21:09.310
And we're now gonna do
with three or four more

00:21:09.310 --> 00:21:11.050
in partnership with that school district,

00:21:11.050 --> 00:21:12.660
we're finding the resources
using a little bit

00:21:12.660 --> 00:21:14.550
of the federal money that's been released,

00:21:14.550 --> 00:21:15.390
and most importantly,

00:21:15.390 --> 00:21:18.610
getting the red tape out of
the way and moving much faster.

00:21:18.610 --> 00:21:22.800
So I'm hoping to be able
to leverage this moment

00:21:22.800 --> 00:21:24.760
to be able to move more rapidly,

00:21:24.760 --> 00:21:26.300
because we know for example,

00:21:26.300 --> 00:21:29.870
every child is now learning
online with Khan Academy,

00:21:29.870 --> 00:21:31.420
because they have connectivity.

00:21:31.420 --> 00:21:34.400
Also has a parent who is
able to find a job online,

00:21:34.400 --> 00:21:37.410
and has a grandparent who is
able to get health information.

00:21:37.410 --> 00:21:39.210
So we know this is critical.

00:21:39.210 --> 00:21:40.510
These are the kinds of disparities

00:21:40.510 --> 00:21:42.060
we have to attack aggressively.

00:21:43.050 --> 00:21:44.313
- And how do you think,

00:21:45.740 --> 00:21:49.670
is COVID, I think in some dimensions,

00:21:49.670 --> 00:21:51.740
it's obviously going to make things worse.

00:21:51.740 --> 00:21:52.890
It's disproportionately folks

00:21:52.890 --> 00:21:54.510
who work in service industries,

00:21:54.510 --> 00:21:55.960
things like that.

00:21:55.960 --> 00:21:58.410
How are you, I guess in the short term,

00:21:58.410 --> 00:21:59.940
I know you have this committee,

00:21:59.940 --> 00:22:01.870
that I've been participating in

00:22:01.870 --> 00:22:05.420
and many other leaders in the area around.

00:22:05.420 --> 00:22:06.610
How do you think we do this,

00:22:06.610 --> 00:22:08.210
with restaurants struggling,

00:22:08.210 --> 00:22:09.990
and obviously that has
following consequences

00:22:09.990 --> 00:22:12.430
with all of the people who work
and supply the restaurants.

00:22:12.430 --> 00:22:15.100
How should the city think
about getting through COVID?

00:22:15.100 --> 00:22:17.343
And then I guess beyond?

00:22:18.310 --> 00:22:20.290
- Yeah in this recovery round table

00:22:20.290 --> 00:22:22.330
that we created here in Silicon Valley,

00:22:22.330 --> 00:22:25.030
and thanks Sal for being a part of this.

00:22:25.030 --> 00:22:29.070
We're really focusing on
how we can most importantly,

00:22:29.070 --> 00:22:31.040
get a lot of folks back to work,

00:22:31.040 --> 00:22:34.170
who may not be getting
back to work otherwise,

00:22:34.170 --> 00:22:36.120
for a year or two based on for example,

00:22:36.120 --> 00:22:37.550
if they're in the hotel industry,

00:22:37.550 --> 00:22:40.920
it's gonna be a long time
before that industry comes back.

00:22:40.920 --> 00:22:43.210
And so we know there's a
lot of low wage workers

00:22:43.210 --> 00:22:45.780
there who critically need skills,

00:22:45.780 --> 00:22:48.260
Khan Academy can help
as we know they are now

00:22:48.260 --> 00:22:51.580
and we want to use whatever
tools we can to help folks

00:22:51.580 --> 00:22:54.203
get rescaled and back working again.

00:22:55.290 --> 00:22:57.990
And part of this again,

00:22:57.990 --> 00:23:01.870
will be around finding
the opportunity in crisis

00:23:01.870 --> 00:23:03.870
as we think about the many businesses

00:23:03.870 --> 00:23:05.740
that can't do business the same way

00:23:05.740 --> 00:23:07.650
because of social distancing.

00:23:07.650 --> 00:23:09.840
They've got a small business
and they can't get customers

00:23:09.840 --> 00:23:13.250
in and out the door at
nearly fast enough rate.

00:23:13.250 --> 00:23:17.500
We announced an Alfresco
initiative to see how

00:23:17.500 --> 00:23:20.460
we can get commerce to happen outside

00:23:20.460 --> 00:23:22.760
in a city with 300 days of sun a year.

00:23:22.760 --> 00:23:25.290
So certainly more outside
seating with restaurants

00:23:25.290 --> 00:23:28.720
by getting yoga studios outside

00:23:28.720 --> 00:23:32.000
and getting retail outside in a way,

00:23:32.000 --> 00:23:36.410
filling up sidewalks and
closing lanes of streets,

00:23:36.410 --> 00:23:38.840
to be able to move our activity outside

00:23:38.840 --> 00:23:40.760
in a way that may actually be lasting

00:23:40.760 --> 00:23:42.680
and maybe something
that's welcoming to folks

00:23:42.680 --> 00:23:45.842
who increasingly are thinking
about cities in different ways

00:23:45.842 --> 00:23:48.040
and that maybe we really want a city

00:23:48.040 --> 00:23:49.880
that is more pedestrian oriented

00:23:49.880 --> 00:23:51.730
and less dominated by the automobile.

00:23:52.980 --> 00:23:53.813
- I love that last point.

00:23:53.813 --> 00:23:55.240
And I do hope it's more permanent.

00:23:55.240 --> 00:23:57.110
And actually I think even schools outside

00:23:57.110 --> 00:23:58.300
is something that I've
always talked about,

00:23:58.300 --> 00:23:59.910
especially out here in California,

00:23:59.910 --> 00:24:00.743
you mentioned 300 days,

00:24:00.743 --> 00:24:02.040
and I think even getting wet a little bit

00:24:02.040 --> 00:24:03.670
on the cold and rainy days

00:24:03.670 --> 00:24:04.910
does something for your character.

00:24:04.910 --> 00:24:07.512
So I think there's something to that.

00:24:07.512 --> 00:24:08.781
Although I haven't heard people talk about

00:24:08.781 --> 00:24:10.350
schools outside yet,

00:24:10.350 --> 00:24:13.440
but I'll just say I think
that'll be great for the kids,

00:24:13.440 --> 00:24:16.110
even if we weren't trying
to socially distance.

00:24:16.110 --> 00:24:17.590
So more questions here.

00:24:17.590 --> 00:24:21.490
This is, it's going back to
the topic where we started,

00:24:21.490 --> 00:24:23.820
but I think it's an
interesting one from Facebook.

00:24:23.820 --> 00:24:25.677
Carol Smith Capper asks,

00:24:25.677 --> 00:24:27.810
"just wondering how
you identify candidates

00:24:27.810 --> 00:24:29.400
for the police department to keep racists

00:24:29.400 --> 00:24:31.860
and people who wanna join
for the sense of power

00:24:31.860 --> 00:24:33.250
it gives them.

00:24:33.250 --> 00:24:35.432
Is there psychological
testing that is done?

00:24:35.432 --> 00:24:38.600
What about as they
progress in their careers?"

00:24:38.600 --> 00:24:40.930
And I'll just add a little
bit to her question,

00:24:40.930 --> 00:24:43.080
which is, and obviously,

00:24:43.080 --> 00:24:44.930
this was in Minneapolis not San Jose,

00:24:44.930 --> 00:24:46.190
and I know you're not the police chief,

00:24:46.190 --> 00:24:48.270
but this was a situation where there was

00:24:48.270 --> 00:24:52.150
multiple complaints against
this officer over the years

00:24:52.150 --> 00:24:55.110
and so why did not other officers there,

00:24:55.110 --> 00:24:56.780
is it that same cultural problems?

00:24:56.780 --> 00:24:59.120
Yeah I'd love anything you know about

00:24:59.120 --> 00:25:01.550
to answer Carol's question.

00:25:01.550 --> 00:25:03.200
- Yeah, great question.

00:25:03.200 --> 00:25:05.170
So as you can imagine,

00:25:05.170 --> 00:25:08.450
super hard to discern who's
a racist and who's not

00:25:08.450 --> 00:25:10.200
when you're signing up for any job.

00:25:11.700 --> 00:25:15.520
And there is psychological testing

00:25:15.520 --> 00:25:17.740
that's done in our department as I expect

00:25:17.740 --> 00:25:21.040
in most other big city departments
as part of the screening,

00:25:21.040 --> 00:25:23.060
when folks are going through the process,

00:25:23.060 --> 00:25:24.740
and it's a very long process,

00:25:24.740 --> 00:25:26.140
I can tell you typically,

00:25:26.140 --> 00:25:28.950
we have 14, 1500 people apply

00:25:28.950 --> 00:25:31.330
to be part of the police academy,

00:25:31.330 --> 00:25:35.280
and about 35 or 40 actually
get into the Academy.

00:25:35.280 --> 00:25:38.693
So we're talking about a
very low acceptance rate.

00:25:39.780 --> 00:25:42.290
And there's a lot of screening.

00:25:42.290 --> 00:25:43.811
Now that being said,

00:25:43.811 --> 00:25:46.510
I think the best thing
police departments can do

00:25:46.510 --> 00:25:49.640
since we're not as human
beings able to discern,

00:25:49.640 --> 00:25:51.340
Malcolm Gladwell actually
just wrote a book

00:25:51.340 --> 00:25:52.173
about this recently.

00:25:52.173 --> 00:25:53.730
We're not very good at discerning

00:25:53.730 --> 00:25:55.520
what strangers really think,

00:25:55.520 --> 00:25:57.410
what they're not telling us.

00:25:57.410 --> 00:26:02.370
So it's really about having
a really effective system

00:26:02.370 --> 00:26:05.530
for identifying the yellow and red flags.

00:26:05.530 --> 00:26:07.513
What is the conduct that's problematic?

00:26:08.790 --> 00:26:11.280
Both with complaints and
what people are seeing

00:26:11.280 --> 00:26:15.080
within the department so that
the internal affairs officers

00:26:15.080 --> 00:26:18.640
are really clearly attuned
to who is a challenge,

00:26:18.640 --> 00:26:20.220
who needs some training?

00:26:20.220 --> 00:26:22.440
Who needs some really clear direction

00:26:22.440 --> 00:26:24.360
and who needs to get out quick.

00:26:24.360 --> 00:26:26.940
And what is real challenge I think,

00:26:26.940 --> 00:26:28.620
in big cities throughout the country

00:26:28.620 --> 00:26:30.230
and certainly in
California as we have this

00:26:30.230 --> 00:26:34.590
very sclerotic system for
actually meeting out discipline

00:26:34.590 --> 00:26:36.950
and firing officers because let's face it,

00:26:36.950 --> 00:26:39.930
police unions are very
powerful in every city

00:26:39.930 --> 00:26:41.910
in every big state.

00:26:41.910 --> 00:26:43.890
And in California we have what's called

00:26:43.890 --> 00:26:45.420
police officers Bill of Rights.

00:26:45.420 --> 00:26:48.410
We have contracts that
are signed in each city

00:26:48.410 --> 00:26:52.170
that make it very difficult
to immediately take action.

00:26:52.170 --> 00:26:53.810
We had an incident very recently,

00:26:53.810 --> 00:26:55.570
an officer who was involved
in the demonstrations

00:26:55.570 --> 00:26:58.703
who was engaged in behavior
that was clearly improper.

00:26:58.703 --> 00:27:00.930
And folks are calling on me to fire him,

00:27:00.930 --> 00:27:03.510
and I literally have no
authority to fire the officer.

00:27:03.510 --> 00:27:06.070
Now I'm not saying that's
what is deserved in this case,

00:27:06.070 --> 00:27:08.450
I think we need to understand
and investigate what happened.

00:27:08.450 --> 00:27:10.560
Clearly discipline is required.

00:27:10.560 --> 00:27:12.520
But I don't have any authority.

00:27:12.520 --> 00:27:15.210
And in fact even the police
chief can't move at that pace

00:27:15.210 --> 00:27:18.890
because of the way it is
restricted by the contract

00:27:18.890 --> 00:27:20.451
and by state law.

00:27:20.451 --> 00:27:22.360
So to get better accountability,

00:27:22.360 --> 00:27:25.020
we need some real changes
in those legal structures.

00:27:25.020 --> 00:27:26.560
And again doesn't happen overnight.

00:27:26.560 --> 00:27:28.820
But it's gotta start happening now.

00:27:28.820 --> 00:27:29.760
- You know the sense that,

00:27:29.760 --> 00:27:31.380
especially with recent incidents,

00:27:31.380 --> 00:27:33.130
that might change because obviously,

00:27:33.130 --> 00:27:37.360
the union is there to represent
the interests of its members

00:27:37.360 --> 00:27:40.252
and obviously these types
of things that happen.

00:27:40.252 --> 00:27:44.360
The people who are at the
receiving end of the brutality,

00:27:44.360 --> 00:27:45.730
they suffer but also,

00:27:45.730 --> 00:27:48.230
other good police officers
suffer incredibly,

00:27:48.230 --> 00:27:50.360
their job becomes that much harder.

00:27:50.360 --> 00:27:52.510
They get exposed to more anger,

00:27:52.510 --> 00:27:54.253
people will trust them less.

00:27:55.410 --> 00:27:57.910
Do you sense that pressure
from within the police force

00:27:57.910 --> 00:27:59.670
and within the unions to say hey look,

00:27:59.670 --> 00:28:02.790
we've gotta take out these actors so that

00:28:02.790 --> 00:28:05.333
all of our jobs are going to be better.

00:28:06.250 --> 00:28:10.070
- I could say nobody hates a
bad cop more than a good cop.

00:28:10.070 --> 00:28:13.500
And I think I really believe
that based on my conversations,

00:28:13.500 --> 00:28:15.200
I used to be a criminal prosecutor

00:28:16.100 --> 00:28:18.460
I prosecuted sexual assault crimes,

00:28:18.460 --> 00:28:21.640
had great relationships
with a lot officers.

00:28:21.640 --> 00:28:23.170
And I can tell you,

00:28:23.170 --> 00:28:26.363
they despise hearing this
stuff and seeing this stuff.

00:28:28.470 --> 00:28:30.563
That being said, I think there are very,

00:28:31.870 --> 00:28:34.190
I'll use the word again
sclerotic political structures

00:28:34.190 --> 00:28:36.450
in place that need change.

00:28:36.450 --> 00:28:38.640
And I had this conversation
with the governor

00:28:38.640 --> 00:28:41.690
and a lot of mayors
just a couple days ago,

00:28:41.690 --> 00:28:44.100
and there is a lot of pent up interest

00:28:44.100 --> 00:28:45.970
among mayors to see this change.

00:28:45.970 --> 00:28:48.740
It's gonna take a lot of pushing.

00:28:48.740 --> 00:28:51.490
And again, this is why I
say people need to register.

00:28:51.490 --> 00:28:53.400
They need to get engaged
and roll up their sleeves,

00:28:53.400 --> 00:28:55.840
'cause this stuff isn't
simple and it doesn't happen.

00:28:55.840 --> 00:28:57.140
You don't get simple solutions

00:28:57.140 --> 00:28:58.490
with tough problems like this.

00:28:58.490 --> 00:29:00.120
It takes a lot of pushing

00:29:02.280 --> 00:29:05.440
- Well that's super
helpful and Mayor Liccardo,

00:29:05.440 --> 00:29:07.850
thanks so much I know
given the circumstances,

00:29:07.850 --> 00:29:09.810
thanks for even continuing to take this.

00:29:09.810 --> 00:29:12.040
I could imagine your life
as you were telling me

00:29:12.040 --> 00:29:13.470
right before, I don't
think you've slept much

00:29:13.470 --> 00:29:14.303
the last couple of days.

00:29:14.303 --> 00:29:16.450
So I really appreciate taking the time out

00:29:16.450 --> 00:29:19.650
for myself and this community
for us to learn about this.

00:29:19.650 --> 00:29:21.640
If you have any other
kind of parting words,

00:29:21.640 --> 00:29:23.370
happy to share them.

00:29:23.370 --> 00:29:24.480
- Well, thanks Sal.

00:29:24.480 --> 00:29:28.000
Thank you for the great work
that you and your partners do.

00:29:28.000 --> 00:29:30.410
And I think we're understanding certainly

00:29:30.410 --> 00:29:33.480
as a result of COVID
the role of Khan Academy

00:29:33.480 --> 00:29:37.710
and distance learning is so
critically vital right now.

00:29:37.710 --> 00:29:39.620
And we know this is
going to be the future.

00:29:39.620 --> 00:29:41.200
So thank you for what you're doing.

00:29:41.200 --> 00:29:44.630
Please continue to support Khan
Academy if you're out there.

00:29:44.630 --> 00:29:46.748
And we look forward to work together

00:29:46.748 --> 00:29:51.010
to see how we can really
solve an awful lot

00:29:51.010 --> 00:29:54.480
of our serious challenges
around equity and race

00:29:55.430 --> 00:29:59.363
and injustice in our
community through education.

00:30:01.000 --> 00:30:03.440
- Well thanks once again for joining.

00:30:03.440 --> 00:30:04.500
I learned a lot,

00:30:04.500 --> 00:30:06.210
And I think there's some
nuance in what you said

00:30:06.210 --> 00:30:09.040
that we're not hearing in
the broader media right now.

00:30:09.040 --> 00:30:11.037
So thank you so much Mayor Liccardo.

00:30:11.037 --> 00:30:13.120
- Sal always a pleasure.

00:30:14.440 --> 00:30:16.980
- So thanks everyone for joining.

00:30:16.980 --> 00:30:20.130
Hopefully you found that
as interesting as I did,

00:30:20.130 --> 00:30:21.466
this is one of those situations where

00:30:21.466 --> 00:30:25.030
I am not an expert in any of this.

00:30:25.030 --> 00:30:27.130
But at Khan Academy,

00:30:27.130 --> 00:30:29.320
we try to pride ourselves
in being curious.

00:30:29.320 --> 00:30:30.990
And this is going to be a learning process

00:30:30.990 --> 00:30:33.570
for all of us of how do we
process all of this information?

00:30:33.570 --> 00:30:35.780
What are the steps that
we can collectively take,

00:30:35.780 --> 00:30:37.520
put one foot in front of the other

00:30:37.520 --> 00:30:40.210
so that we can hopefully
move society forward.

00:30:40.210 --> 00:30:42.220
So thanks everyone, for joining

00:30:42.220 --> 00:30:43.850
and we will continue tomorrow.

00:30:43.850 --> 00:30:46.980
Tomorrow will just be your
standard ask me anything.

00:30:46.980 --> 00:30:49.219
And then I look forward
to having more guests

00:30:49.219 --> 00:30:51.850
like Mayor Liccardo in the weeks to come.

00:30:51.850 --> 00:30:53.263
Thanks everyone for joining.

